WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 put deliberate wrongdoing among the people sources of operational risk. Chapter 7 built the controls — segregation of duties, three-way matching, supplier master file discipline, mandatory leave — that address it. Book 1's Chapter 17 examined earnings quality, which is where financial statement fraud shows up in the numbers. This chapter supplies the conduct those controls are about.
12.1  What business ethics is
Business ethics is the application of moral principles and standards of conduct to the decisions and behaviour of organisations and the people in them. It asks what ought to be done, where law asks what must be done and economics asks what pays.
Four points of definition carry the weight in this syllabus.
It concerns conduct, not sentiment. Ethics is about what is done, not about how anybody feels, and an ethical company is one whose decisions are consistently defensible, not one whose values statement is well written.
It is not the same as legality, and Chapter 15 develops the distinction: much that is legal is unethical, and the gap between the two is where most professional dilemmas live.
It applies to the organisation and to the individual separately. A company has obligations, and so does each professional inside it, and the Statement of Ethical Professional Practice in Chapter 13 binds the individual regardless of what the organisation does.
And it concerns everybody affected by a decision, not only its shareholders. Customers, employees, suppliers, lenders, communities and regulators all have interests, and ethical reasoning means weighing them rather than assuming one of them always wins.
Three approaches to deciding appear in exam answers and are worth having.
Consequences. Which action produces the best overall outcome for everybody affected. Its strength is practicality; its weakness is that it permits harm to a few for the benefit of many and that consequences are forecast rather than known.
Duties and rights. Some things are owed regardless of outcome: tell the truth, keep agreements, respect the rights of others. Its strength is that it does not negotiate; its weakness is that duties conflict.
Character and virtue. What a person of integrity would do, and what the decision makes of the person who takes it. Its strength is that it describes how people actually decide; its weakness is that it gives little guidance in a genuinely new situation.
A practical answer uses all three as tests rather than choosing between them, and adds a fourth that is more useful than any of them in an exam: would this decision survive publication? If an action could not be explained to the people affected, or would be indefensible on the front page of a newspaper, that is strong evidence about it.
