12.2  Fairness and integrity
The outcome names four concepts and asks what each means and how it affects decisions. Two of them are about the character of a decision and two about the care taken over it.
Fairness is treating like cases alike, and treating people according to standards they could accept if they knew them. It has three dimensions the exam uses.
Fairness of outcome: whether the distribution of benefit and burden is defensible. A supplier squeezed on payment terms because it cannot afford to argue is being treated unfairly in outcome, whatever the contract allows.
Fairness of process: whether the decision was made by a method that treats everybody consistently, with the same information and the same opportunity to be heard. A tender awarded on criteria that were changed after bids were received is unfair in process even if the winner was the best bidder.
Fairness of treatment: whether people are dealt with honestly and with respect — told the truth, told in time, not misled by omission.
In practice fairness bites hardest where a company has power: over a small supplier, over a customer who cannot evaluate what is being sold, over an employee who cannot easily leave. The test is not whether the other party agreed; it is whether they agreed on terms they understood and could have refused.
Integrity is wholeness — acting on the same principles consistently, whether or not anybody is watching, and whether or not it is convenient. For a management accountant it has four practical components.
Honesty in what is reported, including what is omitted. A technically true figure presented so as to mislead is a breach of integrity, and Book 1's Chapter 17 showed how much can be done without telling a single lie.
Avoiding conflicts of interest, and disclosing those that cannot be avoided. The conflict is the problem, not the wrongdoing it might lead to, and the obligation arises before anything has gone wrong.
Refusing to do indirectly what may not be done directly — which covers the request to leave something out, to delay a recognition, to book an entry somebody else will reverse.
And consistency between what is said and what is done, which is the component other people actually observe and the one on which a finance function's credibility rests.
Integrity's practical difficulty is that it is tested in small matters long before it is tested in large ones. Nobody is asked to commit a fraud on their first day; they are asked to accept an expense claim they know is wrong, to describe a provision in a way that is technically defensible, to leave a sentence out of a paper. Chapter 14's fraud triangle explains what happens next.
