12.3  Due diligence and fiduciary responsibility
Due diligence is the care a reasonable and competent person would take before acting — the obligation to find out, rather than to assume, before committing the organisation or signing something.
It has a general sense and a specific one, and the exam uses both.
In its general sense, due diligence is the standard of care in any professional act: checking the figures before certifying them, understanding a contract before recommending it, verifying a counterparty before relying on it, reading the file before giving the advice. Its breach is negligence rather than dishonesty, which is why it is the obligation professionals most often fail without noticing.
In its specific sense it is the investigation of a target before an acquisition — the work Book 3's Chapter 18 assumed had been done when it valued Ghab Mills: financial due diligence on the numbers, legal on the contracts and liabilities, commercial on the market and customers, operational on the assets and processes, and increasingly ethical due diligence on conduct, sanctions and bribery exposure.
Three ethical dimensions of due diligence are examinable.
It is owed even where the answer is unwelcome. Due diligence performed to confirm a decision already taken is not due diligence; it is documentation.
Its scope must match the stakes. Reasonable care on a 10,000 contract is not reasonable care on a 10,000,000 one.
And a professional who cannot complete it must say so, rather than signing with an unspoken reservation.
Fiduciary responsibility is the duty owed by someone who acts for another and is trusted with their interests or their property. Directors owe it to the company; trustees to beneficiaries; agents to principals; and a finance professional with authority over funds or information is in a fiduciary position whether or not the word appears anywhere in their contract.
Its content is specific and worth learning as a list.
The duty of loyalty: to act in the beneficiary's interest and not one's own, which is why self-dealing, taking a corporate opportunity for oneself, and undisclosed benefits from suppliers are breaches of a different order from ordinary misconduct.
The duty of care: to act with the competence and diligence of section 12.3's first half.
The duty of good faith: to act honestly and for the purpose for which the authority was given.
The duty of confidentiality: not to use or disclose information obtained in the role for any other purpose — which Chapter 13 makes one of the four IMA standards.
And the duty to account: to be able to show what was done with what was entrusted, which is the ethical foundation of the entire discipline of accounting.
A fiduciary duty is stricter than an ordinary commercial obligation in one specific way the exam likes: the fiduciary must avoid even the possibility of a conflict, and good intentions are not a defence. It is no answer that the transaction was fair, or that the company did not lose; what is objected to is acting for oneself in a matter where one was acting for another.
