12.5  Preventing and detecting
The control methods were set out in Chapter 7; what belongs here is how they map onto the frauds and what ethics adds to them.
Figure F812-02 makes the mapping.
Figure F812-02. Frauds and the controls that address them
@table
Fraud | How it works | Principal control | What it depends on
Skimming | Cash taken before recording | Independent recording at the point of receipt | Two people, or a machine
Inventory theft | Stock removed or written off | Counts, reconciliation of waste, access control | Counts done by people who do not hold the stock
Payroll fraud | Ghost employees, false hours | Separation of payroll records from payments; review of the payroll | Somebody checking a list they did not make
Fictitious supplier | A supplier that exists only in the file | Master file access, bank verification, new supplier review | Restricted access being enforced
Receivable fraud | Lapping, false credit notes, write-offs | Statement reconciliation, authorisation of write-offs, mandatory leave | Rotation and independent confirmation
Statement fraud | Entries, estimates and omissions | Review by somebody competent and independent; audit committee access | Tone at the top, and a route to raise concerns
@end
The last column is the ethics column, and it is the point of this chapter. Every control in the fifth column depends on something in the sixth, and what the sixth column contains is people being willing and able to do their job honestly. A segregation of duties that everybody agrees to work around is not a control; a review performed by somebody who will not question the chief executive is not a review; and a supplier approval process in which no approval has ever been refused is a procedure, not a control.
Three ethical additions to the control framework, which Chapters 16 to 18 develop.
A code of conduct that says specifically what is and is not acceptable, so that nobody has to guess.
A route to raise a concern that does not require going through the person the concern is about, which is Chapter 18's whistleblowing framework.
And a demonstrated willingness to act on what is found, at every level. A fraud by a junior employee that ends in dismissal, and a similar act by a senior one that ends in a quiet resignation, teach everybody the same lesson about which rules apply to whom — and that lesson does more damage than the original act.
