LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Integrity", "fraud" and "irregularity"
Integrity in English carries two senses that are really one: moral uprightness, and structural wholeness — the integrity of a dam. Both mean nothing missing and nothing hollow, which is the right image for the professional sense. Arabic النزاهة and French intégrité carry the moral sense cleanly. Fraud is a legal term as well as an ethical one, and that matters in a report: writing that an employee committed fraud is an allegation of a crime, which is why investigators write irregularity, discrepancy or unexplained difference until a conclusion is reached, and why a management accountant should do the same. The caution is not evasion: it is the difference between describing what is known and asserting what has not been established. Arabic احتيال and French fraude carry the same legal weight, and the same caution applies. Write what you observed, say what it is consistent with, and let the conclusion follow the investigation rather than precede it.
