Chapter summary
Figure F812-04 brings the chapter together.
Figure F812-04. Chapter 12 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Business ethics is the application of moral principles to the conduct of organisations and the people in them: about conduct rather than sentiment, distinct from legality, binding on the individual as well as the organisation, and concerned with everybody a decision affects — tested by consequences, by duties and rights, by character, and practically by whether a decision could be explained to those it affects. Fairness means treating like cases alike in outcome, in process and in treatment, and it bites hardest where a company has power; integrity means acting on the same principles consistently, including honesty about what is omitted, avoidance and disclosure of conflicts, refusal to do indirectly what may not be done directly, and consistency between word and act — tested in small matters long before large ones. Due diligence is the care a reasonable and competent person takes before acting, owed even when the answer is unwelcome and scaled to the stakes; fiduciary responsibility is the duty of one who acts for another, comprising loyalty, care, good faith, confidentiality and the duty to account, and it is breached by self-dealing even where no loss results. Fraud takes three branches: asset misappropriation, the most common, through skimming, larceny, inventory theft, payroll fraud and ghost employees, fictitious suppliers and receivable frauds such as lapping; financial statement fraud, the rarest and most expensive, motivated by targets, covenants and bonuses rather than by direct enrichment; and corruption, where the company is often the beneficiary rather than the victim. And every control that addresses them depends on something a control cannot supply — 261,000 taken by a fictitious supplier needs 261,000 ÷ 0.0707 = 3,691,655 of sales to replace, but what matters more is what the fraud shows about an environment in which people are willing and able to do their jobs honestly.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P12-01  Business ethics is concerned with:
A.	what ought to be done.
B.	what the law requires.
C.	what competitors do.
D.	what maximises profit.
P12-02  Fairness of process concerns:
A.	whether the method treats everybody consistently.
B.	whether the outcome is equal.
C.	whether the result is profitable.
D.	whether the law was followed.
P12-03  Integrity requires that a professional:
A.	act on the same principles whether or not observed.
B.	avoid all risk.
C.	follow instructions.
D.	maximise shareholder value.
P12-04  A conflict of interest should be disclosed:
A.	as soon as it arises.
B.	only after a loss.
C.	only to the external auditor.
D.	only if asked.
P12-05  Due diligence is breached by:
A.	failing to find out what a reasonable professional would have found out.
B.	dishonesty alone.
C.	any loss.
D.	disagreeing with management.
P12-06  Fiduciary duty includes all of the following except:
A.	a duty to maximise short-term profit.
B.	a duty of loyalty.
C.	a duty of care.
D.	a duty to account.
P12-07  Skimming is:
A.	taking cash before it is recorded.
B.	taking recorded cash.
C.	creating a false supplier.
D.	overstating revenue.
P12-08  A ghost employee is a form of:
A.	payroll fraud.
B.	receivable fraud.
C.	statement fraud.
D.	corruption.
P12-09  Lapping covers a stolen balance by:
A.	applying a later customer's receipt to it.
B.	writing it off.
C.	issuing a credit note.
D.	capitalising it.
P12-10  Financial statement fraud is usually motivated by:
A.	a target, covenant, bonus or reputation.
B.	direct personal theft.
C.	inventory shortages.
D.	supplier pressure.
P12-11  Loss of 261,000 at a net margin of 7.07 per cent requires replacement sales of about:
A.	3,691,655
B.	18,453
C.	261,000
D.	369,166
P12-12  Corruption differs from the other branches in that:
A.	the company is often the beneficiary rather than the victim.
B.	it is always small.
C.	it leaves no trace.
D.	it is never illegal.
Case-style practice: the professional fees
Orontes's internal auditor notices that payments to a consultancy called Levant Advisory have totalled 261,000 over two years, in eighteen invoices averaging 14,500, each just below the 15,000 threshold requiring a second approval. The supplier was created in the master file by the assistant financial controller, who also approves invoices below the threshold. The bank details on the file match no bank branch near the address given. No contract or deliverable can be found.
Required. Say what this pattern is consistent with, what you would do next and in what order, and which controls failed. Do not assert that a fraud has occurred. Under 350 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC12-1  Answer A. Ethics asks what ought to be done.
B is wrong. That is law.
C is wrong. That is economics.
D is wrong. Custom is evidence, not a standard.
SC12-2  Answer A. Defensible decisions, consistently.
B is wrong. A statement is not conduct.
C is wrong. Profit level is not the test.
D is wrong. Feeling is not conduct.
SC12-3  Answer A. Explicability to those affected.
B is wrong. Legality is a separate test.
C is wrong. Profit is not the question.
D is wrong. Custom does not justify.
SC12-4  Answer A. The method was not applied consistently.
B is wrong. No fiduciary relationship is at issue.
C is wrong. Care was not the failing.
D is wrong. The outcome does not cure the process.
SC12-5  Answer A. Misleading is a breach of integrity.
B is wrong. Rules are a floor, not the standard.
C is wrong. Care was exercised; honesty was not.
D is wrong. It is primarily an honesty failure.
SC12-6  Answer A. On arising, before harm.
B is wrong. The duty does not wait for loss.
C is wrong. Timing is immediate.
D is wrong. The duty is not conditional on a rule.
SC12-7  Answer A. It is documentation.
B is wrong. Completeness does not cure the purpose.
C is wrong. Due diligence is a duty of care.
D is wrong. Standards require genuine inquiry.
SC12-8  Answer A. Self-interest in another's matter.
B is wrong. Risk-taking is permitted.
C is wrong. Reporting to the board is required.
D is wrong. Resignation may be the remedy.
SC12-9  Answer A. The breach is the conflict, not the loss.
B is wrong. The duty was breached.
C is wrong. Loyalty, not care, failed.
D is wrong. Disclosure afterwards does not cure it.
SC12-10  Answer A. Nothing was recorded to compare against.
B is wrong. Skimming amounts are usually small.
C is wrong. One person suffices.
D is wrong. It is theft.
SC12-11  Answer A. Receipts are rolled between customer accounts.
B is wrong. Payroll frauds use employees.
C is wrong. Inventory frauds use goods.
D is wrong. Reporting fraud misstates the accounts.
SC12-12  Answer A. Rarest and costliest.
B is wrong. Misappropriation is the common one.
C is wrong. The motive is usually indirect.
D is wrong. Audits reduce but do not eliminate it.
SC12-13  Answer A. A control worked around is not a control.
B is wrong. Its effect is nil, not reduced.
C is wrong. Segregation is preventive.
D is wrong. Documentation does not perform it.
SC12-14  Answer A. 2,969,100 ÷ 42,000,000 = 7.07 per cent.
B is wrong. That doubles it.
C is wrong. That is ten times too large.
D is wrong. That is ten times too small.
SC12-15  Answer A. Inconsistency teaches the wrong lesson.
B is wrong. The long-run cost exceeds any saving.
C is wrong. It damages the control environment.
D is wrong. Confidentiality does not require inconsistency.
