Treating like cases alike on acceptable standards | Fairness.
Acting on the same principles whether or not observed | Integrity.
The care a reasonable, competent person takes | Due diligence.
The duty of one who acts for another | Fiduciary responsibility.
Using a later receipt to cover an earlier theft | Lapping, a receivable fraud.
Sales needed to replace 261,000 at 7.07 per cent | 3,691,655: 261,000 ÷ 0.0707 = 3,691,655.
What it tells you | Four concepts and two frauds — and the four concepts are what the controls against the two frauds ultimately rest on.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P12-01  Answer A. What ought to be done.
B is wrong. That is law.
C is wrong. Custom is not a standard.
D is wrong. Profit is not the criterion.
P12-02  Answer A. Consistency of method.
B is wrong. That is fairness of outcome.
C is wrong. Profit is irrelevant.
D is wrong. Legality is separate.
P12-03  Answer A. Consistency, observed or not.
B is wrong. Risk is not the subject.
C is wrong. Instructions may be improper.
D is wrong. That is an objective, not a principle of conduct.
P12-04  Answer A. Immediately.
B is wrong. Loss is not the trigger.
C is wrong. Internal disclosure comes first.
D is wrong. The duty is not reactive.
P12-05  Answer A. A failure of inquiry.
B is wrong. Dishonesty breaches integrity.
C is wrong. Loss alone proves nothing.
D is wrong. Disagreement is not a breach.
P12-06  Answer A. No such fiduciary duty exists.
B is wrong. Loyalty is central.
C is wrong. Care is central.
D is wrong. Accounting is central.
P12-07  Answer A. Before recording.
B is wrong. That is larceny.
C is wrong. That is a disbursement fraud.
D is wrong. That is statement fraud.
P12-08  Answer A. Payroll.
B is wrong. Receivables involve customers.
C is wrong. Statements involve reporting.
D is wrong. Corruption involves outsiders.
P12-09  Answer A. A later receipt covers the gap.
B is wrong. A write-off is a different method.
C is wrong. So is a credit note.
D is wrong. Capitalising is a reporting manipulation.
P12-10  Answer A. Indirect motives dominate.
B is wrong. Direct theft characterises misappropriation.
C is wrong. Inventory is a different branch.
D is wrong. Suppliers are not the usual motive.
P12-11  Answer A. 261,000 ÷ 0.0707 = 3,691,655.
B is wrong. That multiplies instead of dividing.
C is wrong. That is the loss itself.
D is wrong. That is ten times too small.
P12-12  Answer A. The company may gain from it.
B is wrong. Corruption can be very large.
C is wrong. It leaves traces, if often outside the company.
D is wrong. It is illegal in most jurisdictions.
Case-style set: the professional fees
What the pattern is consistent with. Four features point the same way. Payments to a supplier created by the person who also approves them is a failure of segregation of duties. Eighteen invoices averaging 14,500, each just below a 15,000 second-approval threshold, is a pattern consistent with deliberate structuring rather than with coincidence. Bank details matching no branch near the stated address is a discrepancy requiring explanation. And the absence of any contract or deliverable means nothing has been received for 261,000. Taken together these are consistent with a fictitious supplier arrangement. They are not proof: there may be an explanation, and it is not my role to conclude one way or the other at this stage.
What I would do next, in order. First, preserve the evidence: secure the master file change log, the approval records, the invoices and the bank instructions, before anything can be altered. Second, restrict the ability to alter the supplier record and to approve payments to it, without announcing why, since further payments would increase the loss. Third, report to the audit committee or its chair, not to the financial controller, because the assistant financial controller's line management is within the area in question. Fourth, obtain advice on how to proceed — legal, and on handling evidence — before anybody is interviewed. Fifth, establish the full extent: every supplier created by the same person, every payment just under a threshold, and every bank detail changed in the period.
Which controls failed. Master file access was not restricted to people who do not approve payments. Bank details were not verified independently to a number held on file. New suppliers were not reviewed against employee records. The approval threshold was applied mechanically, with no review of the pattern of invoices just below it. No contract or deliverable was required before payment for services. And no exception report on new or low-activity suppliers reached anybody independent.
One further point for the audit committee. The amount is 261,000, which at Orontes's net margin of 7.07 per cent is 261,000 ÷ 0.0707 = 3,691,655 of sales. But the material question is not this amount; it is what else could have happened under the same conditions, and over what period.
